{
  "id": "editor-grid",
  "title": "A building seat's grid lies on the working plane at the platform's height, or on every surface, and a hidden grid leaves the frame as it was",
  "binding": "The positive leg boots an offscreen 256x144 world whose seat looks from 0,6,9 at 0,0.5,0 over four red solids: a floor slab with its top at height 0, a two-unit-square platform with its top at height 1 left of centre, a ramp rising 25 degrees away from the viewer right of centre, and a wall facing the viewer behind them. Every capture is awaited with world.wait captures before the next is armed, and none misses its deadline. With no grid the scene is captured as plain.png. The seat enters build mode (player.build), sets a one-unit pitch and a working plane at height 1, and switches the grid on: plane.png shows blue grid lines on the platform's top, while the floor, a unit under the plane's band, shows none. In surface mode (surface.png) the lattice lies on every surface, projected along each surface's normal. Each region's bound is the region's mean blue, read on both backends alike: the platform reads 0.485 with the grid and 0.141 without, bounded at 0.35; the floor 0.323 and 0.140, bounded at 0.25; the ramp 0.277 and 0.140, bounded at 0.22; the wall 0.285 and 0.074, bounded at 0.19. The floor with the plane's grid reads its grid-free 0.140 and is held at most 0.18, below the 0.25 its grid must reach. With the grid switched off, off.png agrees with plain.png. The discriminating leg runs the same verbs without player.build, so the seat plays: none of its views draws a grid, every grid-present observation fails, and surface.png agrees with plain.png.",
  "bootShape": "offscreen",
  "backends": ["vulkan", "directx"],
  "requirements": ["gpu"],
  "timeoutSeconds": 60,
  "positive": {
